# DGX OS — history

## Origin (2016)

DGX OS shipped with the first DGX-1 (P100 Pascal, 8x GPU, NVLink 1.0).
Ubuntu Server LTS underneath, with an NVIDIA overlay: preinstalled CUDA,
cuDNN, NCCL, GPU driver, Docker + `nvidia-docker` runtime, and a
curated set of deep-learning framework containers pulled from what
became NGC (NVIDIA GPU Cloud). The pitch was "unbox, plug in,
`docker run` a TensorFlow container." At the time, getting a matched
driver + CUDA + framework stack working on bare metal was a multi-day
chore; DGX OS existed so buyers of a $129K box didn't have to do it.

## DGX OS 3.x (2017-ish)

Ubuntu 16.04 base. Tracked DGX-1 and then DGX Station (Volta,
water-cooled desk-side box). Introduced `nvidia-docker2` support.
NVIDIA started publishing a proper release-notes cadence.

## DGX OS 4.x (2018–2019)

Still 16.04 base, extended for DGX-2 (16x V100 with NVSwitch). Container
runtime moved to the CLI-less `nvidia-container-toolkit` model that
upstream Docker eventually adopted. Firmware / BMC update tooling
matured — DGX OS started shipping vendor firmware bundles you'd
`apt install`.

## DGX OS 5.x (2020–2022)

Rebased on Ubuntu 18.04, then 20.04. Coincided with DGX A100 (Ampere,
MIG — multi-instance GPU). Added MIG partitioning tooling, GPUDirect
Storage bits, and the `nvidia-fabricmanager` service required for
NVSwitch systems. Around this era NVIDIA also started shipping a
separate flavor called **DGX Base OS** — a slimmer image intended for
customers who wanted to run Kubernetes / Slurm / their own orchestrator
on top instead of the batteries-included DGX OS.

## DGX OS 6.x (2022–2024)

Ubuntu 22.04 LTS base. Tracks DGX H100 (Hopper) and the DGX SuperPOD
reference architecture. Ships confidential-compute / TEE support for
Hopper, deeper BlueField DPU integration, and the current-gen
`nvidia-container-toolkit`. This is the version most on-prem DGX
customers are running today.

## DGX OS 7.x (2024–2025)

Ubuntu 24.04 LTS base. Tracks DGX H200 and DGX B200 (Blackwell). Adds
Blackwell driver branch, updated fabricmanager for the new NVLink
switch topology, and integrations with NVIDIA Mission Control / Base
Command for cluster management.

## Parallel product lines worth knowing

- **DGX Cloud (2023+).** Not the same product — hosted DGX instances
  on OCI/Azure/GCP/AWS. Different image, managed by NVIDIA.
- **DGX Spark / DGX Station (GB10, 2025).** The desktop-class
  Grace-Blackwell boxes (arm64, unified memory). These ship
  **DGX OS for DGX Spark**, which is a distinct arm64 build — same
  DGX-OS-branded stack, but a separate release track from the x86
  datacenter DGX OS.
- **NVIDIA AI Enterprise.** Separate SKU: the same container + driver
  stack, but supported on generic servers running RHEL / Ubuntu /
  vSphere. DGX OS is essentially the "we control the whole box"
  cousin.

## Themes across the arc

- Always Ubuntu Server LTS underneath. Never a fork — NVIDIA's overlay
  is drivers, firmware tooling, container runtime, fabricmanager, and
  a curated apt repo, all installable on top.
- The container-first workflow has been the identity of DGX OS since
  day one — the OS's job is to get you from bare metal to
  `docker run nvcr.io/...` with no yak-shaving.
- Release cadence tracks the hardware, not Ubuntu. A new GPU
  generation ⇒ new major DGX OS.
